          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My cholesterol came back at 6.4.",
          "answer": "A total cholesterol of 6.4 is a result to discuss with a clinician. The guidance I can access does not say what 6.4 means for you. The search returned no treatment threshold for total cholesterol in mmol/L.\n\nThe most relevant document is \"Lipids: screening intervals, risk stratification and statin initiation\" (major cardiology society consensus guidance). It says:\n\n- A full lipid panel includes total cholesterol, LDL cholesterol, HDL cholesterol and triglycerides. A non-fasting sample is acceptable for initial screening in most adults.\n- The decision about treatment is driven mainly by your estimated 10-year risk of heart and blood vessel disease, not by one cholesterol number alone.\n\nThe passages did not cover what level of risk calls for action, or how your age, blood pressure, smoking, diabetes and family history change the picture. I can't fill that gap from general knowledge.\n\nConsider asking a clinician:\n- What this 6.4 means for me, given my other results and risk factors.\n- Whether I need my LDL, HDL and triglyceride values too.\n- Whether my 10-year cardiovascular risk should be calculated.\n- How often I should be retested.\n\nDo not start, stop or change any medication based on this number alone.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n\nI also removed my earlier comment that 6.4 is \"above the range many people aim for.\" None of the retrieved passages said that, so I could not ground it.",
